WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.825
Go gives application teams useful security
foundations.

2
00:00:02.905 --> 00:00:05.326
The language encourages clear control
flow, static typing,

3
00:00:05.406 --> 00:00:07.684
explicit error handling, simple deployment
artifacts,

4
00:00:07.764 --> 00:00:10.943
and standard library support for HTTP
services, cryptography, file handling,

5
00:00:11.023 --> 00:00:11.735
and concurrency.

6
00:00:11.815 --> 00:00:14.947
Those strengths can make secure design
easier to express and easier to review.

7
00:00:15.027 --> 00:00:17.353
But Go does not automatically make an
application secure.

8
00:00:17.433 --> 00:00:18.951
Runtime input can still be malformed.

9
00:00:19.031 --> 00:00:20.502
Authorization can still be missing.

10
00:00:20.582 --> 00:00:22.054
Secrets can still leak through logs.

11
00:00:22.134 --> 00:00:23.747
Dependencies can still introduce risk.

12
00:00:23.827 --> 00:00:26.295
A handler can still trust the wrong value,
write too much data,

13
00:00:26.375 --> 00:00:27.751
or fail unpredictably under load.

14
00:00:27.831 --> 00:00:30.536
Security comes from how the application is
designed, implemented,

15
00:00:30.616 --> 00:00:32.040
configured, tested, and operated.

16
00:00:32.120 --> 00:00:34.445
Go applications often run as HTTP APIs,
backend services,

17
00:00:34.525 --> 00:00:37.468
command-line tools, workers, cloud
functions, and internal platforms.

18
00:00:37.548 --> 00:00:39.968
Each context has its own trust boundaries
and failure modes.

19
00:00:40.048 --> 00:00:43.085
A secure Go mindset starts by asking where
data enters, who is allowed to act,

20
00:00:43.165 --> 00:00:46.012
what code makes the trust decision, what
happens when something fails,

21
00:00:46.092 --> 00:00:48.181
and how production behavior can be
observed safely.

22
00:00:48.261 --> 00:00:50.396
The practical goal is to make safe
behavior explicit.

23
00:00:50.476 --> 00:00:51.947
Validate data before sensitive use.

24
00:00:52.027 --> 00:00:53.926
Enforce authorization in trusted server
code.

25
00:00:54.006 --> 00:00:55.714
Handle errors without leaking internals.

26
00:00:55.794 --> 00:00:57.835
Use context, timeouts, and cleanup
consistently.

27
00:00:57.915 --> 00:00:59.243
Review dependencies and builds.

28
00:00:59.323 --> 00:01:00.700
When these habits are repeatable,

29
00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:03.912
secure Go development becomes ordinary
engineering rather than a late-stage

30
00:01:03.992 --> 00:01:04.419
scramble.

31
00:01:04.499 --> 00:01:07.299
This course uses that mindset across
common Go application surfaces.

32
00:01:07.379 --> 00:01:08.945
Instead of memorizing isolated rules,

33
00:01:09.025 --> 00:01:11.635
focus on questions that travel well from
one codebase to another.

34
00:01:11.715 --> 00:01:12.712
What values are trusted?

35
00:01:12.792 --> 00:01:13.931
What values are only parsed?

36
00:01:14.011 --> 00:01:15.244
Which component owns cleanup?

37
00:01:15.324 --> 00:01:18.077
What evidence would show that the control
still works after a change?

38
00:01:18.157 --> 00:01:20.340
These questions make review conversations
concrete.
